WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.025 --> 00:00:01.226
Ann Hohenhaus:
"One human year equals

00:00:01.226 --> 00:00:02.694
seven dog years."

00:00:02.694 --> 00:00:05.363
Myth. Myth.
We could cut it right there.

00:00:05.363 --> 00:00:07.632
Carly Fox: "Certain dog
breeds are hypoallergenic

00:00:07.632 --> 00:00:08.900
because of their fur."

00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:10.402
This is a major, major myth.

00:00:10.402 --> 00:00:12.670
"Only male dogs hump."

00:00:12.670 --> 00:00:15.707
Hi, I'm Dr. Ann Hohenhaus,
I'm the staff veterinarian

00:00:15.707 --> 00:00:17.242
at the
Animal Medical Center

00:00:17.242 --> 00:00:18.176
here in New York City.

00:00:18.176 --> 00:00:21.346
My name is Dr. Carly
Fox, and I'm an emergency

00:00:21.346 --> 00:00:23.048
and critical care
staff veterinarian

00:00:23.048 --> 00:00:24.382
at the
Animal Medical Center.

00:00:24.382 --> 00:00:26.951
Hohenhaus: Today we're
debunking dog myths.

00:00:26.951 --> 00:00:28.920
Fox: I think, honestly,

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:32.524
the majority of male dogs

00:00:32.524 --> 00:00:35.727
do like to hump,
but I guess female dogs

00:00:35.727 --> 00:00:38.296
also can be seen  to
exhibit that behavior.

00:00:38.296 --> 00:00:40.198
Hohenhaus: But males
hump more than females.

00:00:40.198 --> 00:00:42.300
Fox: Definitely, yeah.
But, I mean,

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:44.002
I guess female dogs
can also hump,

00:00:44.002 --> 00:00:45.570
I mean, if they're
in the mood.

00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:47.305
Hohenhaus: Next myth:
"Dogs are colorblind."

00:00:47.305 --> 00:00:49.908
Dogs are
partially colorblind.

00:00:49.908 --> 00:00:51.910
People used to think they
were totally colorblind.

00:00:51.910 --> 00:00:54.345
Dogs have a little bit
of ability to see color,

00:00:54.345 --> 00:00:55.280
and so they see things

00:00:55.280 --> 00:00:57.048
in kind
of blue, yellow, and gray.

00:00:57.048 --> 00:00:58.950
But they don't see
a lot of color.

00:00:58.950 --> 00:01:01.553
Fox: "Dogs don't like
to be hugged."

00:01:01.553 --> 00:01:03.955
I mean, biggest myth.

00:01:03.955 --> 00:01:05.757
Physical touch is
one of the things that

00:01:05.757 --> 00:01:08.927
makes a dog a dog,
so I can't imagine 

00:01:08.927 --> 00:01:10.328
that dogs don't
like to be hugged.

00:01:10.328 --> 00:01:12.964
I mean, dogs will literally
climb onto your laps

00:01:12.964 --> 00:01:15.967
in order to be hugged, so
this is an absolute myth.

00:01:15.967 --> 00:01:17.335
You can't and shouldn't just

00:01:17.335 --> 00:01:19.571
go up to any dog and hug it.

00:01:19.571 --> 00:01:23.174
Dogs still are different,
just like people are.

00:01:23.174 --> 00:01:25.310
Some people are huggers,
some people are not.

00:01:25.310 --> 00:01:30.014
So some dogs don't love
that very direct attention

00:01:30.014 --> 00:01:31.349
from someone perhaps
they don't know,

00:01:31.349 --> 00:01:32.717
so be sure to
ask the owner

00:01:32.717 --> 00:01:34.519
if you can pet or
hug their dog,

00:01:34.519 --> 00:01:36.521
and read the dog's signs.

00:01:36.521 --> 00:01:38.490
Hohenhaus: Next myth:
"One human year

00:01:38.490 --> 00:01:40.291
equals seven dog years."

00:01:40.291 --> 00:01:42.894
Myth. Myth.
We could cut it right there,

00:01:42.894 --> 00:01:45.230
but there's some really
interesting research

00:01:45.230 --> 00:01:46.531
behind this question.

00:01:46.531 --> 00:01:49.134
So, the seven years
probably comes

00:01:49.134 --> 00:01:51.236
when people lived
to be about 70

00:01:51.236 --> 00:01:52.737
and dogs lived
to be about 10,

00:01:52.737 --> 00:01:54.606
so 70 divided
by 10 is seven.

00:01:54.606 --> 00:01:56.574
That's where that number
maybe comes from.

00:01:56.574 --> 00:01:57.809
Nobody knows, it's a myth,

00:01:57.809 --> 00:01:59.377
so there's
no data behind it.

00:01:59.377 --> 00:02:00.845
Then, a number
of years ago,

00:02:00.845 --> 00:02:03.681
a group of researchers
at Purdue University

00:02:03.681 --> 00:02:06.651
looked at developing
a formula for dog age

00:02:06.651 --> 00:02:08.786
based on the
size of the dog

00:02:08.786 --> 00:02:10.588
and the dog's
chronologic age,

00:02:10.588 --> 00:02:12.457
or how many calendar
years it was old.

00:02:12.457 --> 00:02:15.193
And that formula might
actually be the best.

00:02:15.193 --> 00:02:17.228
This is a myth,
but there are ways

00:02:17.228 --> 00:02:19.397
that you can calculate
your dog's age

00:02:19.397 --> 00:02:21.966
based on a complicated
mathematical formula.

00:02:21.966 --> 00:02:23.835
Next one.
Fox: Oh, OK.

00:02:23.835 --> 00:02:27.805
"You should shave fluffy
dogs in the summer."

00:02:27.805 --> 00:02:29.574
This is mostly a myth.

00:02:29.574 --> 00:02:31.809
A lot of people will shave
their dogs in the summer

00:02:31.809 --> 00:02:33.678
because they think it
will make them cooler.

00:02:33.678 --> 00:02:35.580
That's not necessarily true.

00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:38.183
Dogs have adapted
to all types of weather,

00:02:38.183 --> 00:02:41.319
and they actually will shed

00:02:41.319 --> 00:02:42.921
different coats at
different times of the year

00:02:42.921 --> 00:02:43.955
in order to sort of be

00:02:43.955 --> 00:02:45.390
comfortable in
that weather.

00:02:45.390 --> 00:02:47.025
Hohenhaus: I think they
don't like to be shaved

00:02:47.025 --> 00:02:49.294
because they feel naked.

00:02:49.294 --> 00:02:51.729
Fox: Yeah, probably.
I mean,

00:02:51.729 --> 00:02:53.531
why wouldn't they?
They have no idea.

00:02:53.531 --> 00:02:55.400
Hohenhaus: Yeah, they've
never not had hair before.

00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:56.935
So I'm not sure
that shaving them

00:02:56.935 --> 00:02:59.437
is always a good thing
either, so myth.

00:02:59.437 --> 00:03:01.973
"Dog saliva
can heal wounds."

00:03:01.973 --> 00:03:04.842
When's the last time
you got bitten by a dog?

00:03:04.842 --> 00:03:06.244
Fox: Probably,
like, last year.

00:03:06.244 --> 00:03:07.946
Hohenhaus: And it's ugly.

00:03:07.946 --> 00:03:11.115
It is not healing wounds;
it's making wounds worse.

00:03:11.115 --> 00:03:13.451
This is a total, total myth.

00:03:13.451 --> 00:03:16.487
Dog bites are really
nothing to laugh about,

00:03:16.487 --> 00:03:19.958
and most veterinarians
get bit on the hand,

00:03:19.958 --> 00:03:21.492
and so we're
always worried

00:03:21.492 --> 00:03:22.927
because we work
with our hands,

00:03:22.927 --> 00:03:25.396
so any dog bite needs
to see a veterinarian

00:03:25.396 --> 00:03:28.499
because it's a potentially
infected injury,

00:03:28.499 --> 00:03:31.002
and you need to go
to your physician

00:03:31.002 --> 00:03:32.437
to have that bite looked at.

00:03:32.437 --> 00:03:34.706
Fox: "Certain dog breeds
are hypoallergenic

00:03:34.706 --> 00:03:35.940
because of their fur."

00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:37.408
This is a major, major myth.

00:03:37.408 --> 00:03:39.644
So, all dogs
produce allergens,

00:03:39.644 --> 00:03:40.545
and some dogs
will produce

00:03:40.545 --> 00:03:42.013
more allergens than others.

00:03:42.013 --> 00:03:44.115
Dogs that don't shed,
like poodles,

00:03:44.115 --> 00:03:46.451
or smaller dogs
that shed less

00:03:46.451 --> 00:03:49.821
are less likely to produce
a large amount of allergen

00:03:49.821 --> 00:03:51.956
that can cause allergies
in the environment,

00:03:51.956 --> 00:03:55.026
and it really has to do
with how often they shed

00:03:55.026 --> 00:03:56.261
or if they shed at all.

00:03:56.261 --> 00:03:58.196
But definitely all dogs
produce allergens,

00:03:58.196 --> 00:04:00.632
and all dogs
could be allergenic,

00:04:00.632 --> 00:04:02.133
but some dogs
will produce less.

00:04:02.133 --> 00:04:03.701
Hohenhaus: And now we
have a different myth:

00:04:03.701 --> 00:04:06.704
"You can't teach
an old dog new tricks."

00:04:06.704 --> 00:04:09.941
I think that's an idiom
rather than a myth, isn't it?

00:04:09.941 --> 00:04:11.242
Some famous saying.

00:04:11.242 --> 00:04:13.044
I think that that is a myth.

00:04:13.044 --> 00:04:14.512
Training a dog
is never easy,

00:04:14.512 --> 00:04:16.481
and it involves repetition

00:04:16.481 --> 00:04:18.516
and persistence
and rewards

00:04:18.516 --> 00:04:20.418
in order to
successfully train a dog,

00:04:20.418 --> 00:04:22.287
but I don't think
age is the problem.

00:04:22.287 --> 00:04:24.322
I think we're
more of the problem

00:04:24.322 --> 00:04:26.291
in not sticking with
training a dog.

00:04:26.291 --> 00:04:28.326
Fox: Yes, they actually
did a study about this,

00:04:28.326 --> 00:04:32.163
and they did the same
teaching of a trick

00:04:32.163 --> 00:04:33.831
to young dogs
and old dogs,

00:04:33.831 --> 00:04:36.968
dogs that were
six months to a year

00:04:36.968 --> 00:04:38.703
and then dogs that
were 10 years old.

00:04:38.703 --> 00:04:39.804
And they
actually found out

00:04:39.804 --> 00:04:43.408
that both sets of dogs
can learn a new trick.

00:04:43.408 --> 00:04:45.376
However, the dogs
that were older,

00:04:45.376 --> 00:04:47.245
it took them twice
as long to learn it.

00:04:47.245 --> 00:04:49.414
So they can learn it,
it's just something

00:04:49.414 --> 00:04:51.182
that requires more time,

00:04:51.182 --> 00:04:52.650
and that's definitely
something that

00:04:52.650 --> 00:04:54.352
you are going to
have to commit to.

00:04:54.352 --> 00:04:55.720
But it's possible,

00:04:55.720 --> 00:04:57.789
and they definitely
can learn new tricks.

00:04:57.789 --> 00:04:59.390
Hohenhaus:
"Dogs feel guilt."

00:04:59.390 --> 00:05:03.094
I think that humans
want the dog to feel guilt

00:05:03.094 --> 00:05:05.096
for making a mess,
chewing the furniture,

00:05:05.096 --> 00:05:06.597
taking the fringe
off the sofa,

00:05:06.597 --> 00:05:07.632
destroying the pillows,

00:05:07.632 --> 00:05:10.068
'cause we want
them to be like us.

00:05:10.068 --> 00:05:12.003
So, I don't think dogs

00:05:12.003 --> 00:05:14.339
necessarily can
really feel guilt,

00:05:14.339 --> 00:05:15.807
but since
they're not talking,

00:05:15.807 --> 00:05:18.009
it's really hard to know
if that's true or not.

00:05:18.009 --> 00:05:21.346
Fox: Our dogs will display
guilt-like behavior

00:05:21.346 --> 00:05:22.513
when they do
something wrong,

00:05:22.513 --> 00:05:24.248
like, they'll put their
ears back, they'll hide,

00:05:24.248 --> 00:05:25.717
they'll look very guilty.

00:05:25.717 --> 00:05:27.752
But I think in most cases,

00:05:27.752 --> 00:05:29.487
that's really a
reaction to you

00:05:29.487 --> 00:05:31.289
and your tone of voice

00:05:31.289 --> 00:05:33.024
or your anger at them

00:05:33.024 --> 00:05:35.326
and your body language

00:05:35.326 --> 00:05:36.461
rather than true guilt.

00:05:36.461 --> 00:05:37.562
Hohenhaus: One of
the reasons

00:05:37.562 --> 00:05:38.596
that dogs have been

00:05:38.596 --> 00:05:41.366
so effective in integrating

00:05:41.366 --> 00:05:43.701
themselves into our lives,

00:05:43.701 --> 00:05:46.371
the lives of
humans for millennia,

00:05:46.371 --> 00:05:48.106
is because they
are very good

00:05:48.106 --> 00:05:50.141
at picking up
on social cues.

00:05:50.141 --> 00:05:52.443
Fox: "When dogs
wag their tails,

00:05:52.443 --> 00:05:53.811
it means they're happy."

00:05:53.811 --> 00:05:55.246
I would say in
a lot of times

00:05:55.246 --> 00:05:57.181
when dogs wag their tails,

00:05:57.181 --> 00:05:59.183
it seems like
they are very happy.

00:05:59.183 --> 00:06:02.520
However, tail wagging
is a behavior in dogs

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:03.821
that can mean
many different things,

00:06:03.821 --> 00:06:05.490
not necessarily happiness.

00:06:05.490 --> 00:06:07.658
Hohenhaus: And I think
the wag matters.

00:06:07.658 --> 00:06:10.928
So you've got a dog whose
tail is quivering like this,

00:06:10.928 --> 00:06:12.697
that is not a happy wag.

00:06:12.697 --> 00:06:15.199
Think about the Labrador,
swish, swish, swish, swish,

00:06:15.199 --> 00:06:16.467
they're pretty much
always happy.

00:06:16.467 --> 00:06:18.936
And then there's the dog
who their tail quivers

00:06:18.936 --> 00:06:20.438
and then it curls under.

00:06:20.438 --> 00:06:21.739
That's not a
happy wag either.

00:06:21.739 --> 00:06:23.574
That's a
"I'm really upset" wag.

00:06:23.574 --> 00:06:26.444
So in addition to
looking at the ears

00:06:26.444 --> 00:06:28.646
and the eyes and
the body posture,

00:06:28.646 --> 00:06:30.815
ask yourself, what kind
of wag is this dog doing?

00:06:30.815 --> 00:06:32.917
Because that's gonna
give you an indication

00:06:32.917 --> 00:06:34.752
on the mood of the beast.

00:06:34.752 --> 00:06:36.888
Fox: "Human food
is bad for dogs."

00:06:36.888 --> 00:06:38.289
Well, there are
very particular

00:06:38.289 --> 00:06:39.424
types of human food

00:06:39.424 --> 00:06:41.526
that are definitively
bad for dogs.

00:06:41.526 --> 00:06:43.928
For example, chocolate is
obviously bad for dogs.

00:06:43.928 --> 00:06:46.431
Grapes, raisins
are toxic to dogs.

00:06:46.431 --> 00:06:49.133
Onions, garlic, those
things are bad for dogs.

00:06:49.133 --> 00:06:50.968
However,
human food in general

00:06:50.968 --> 00:06:52.970
is not necessarily
bad for dogs.

00:06:52.970 --> 00:06:55.006
We do wanna always
limit the quantity

00:06:55.006 --> 00:06:56.507
of human food for dogs.

00:06:56.507 --> 00:06:59.277
I usually tell people less
than 10% of their diet

00:06:59.277 --> 00:07:00.445
should be human food.

00:07:00.445 --> 00:07:02.980
Just human food
in moderation

00:07:02.980 --> 00:07:04.615
is not bad for dogs.

00:07:04.615 --> 00:07:06.184
Hohenhaus: Well,
dog food is

00:07:06.184 --> 00:07:07.885
good balanced
meal for a dog.

00:07:07.885 --> 00:07:10.221
I interpret it to mean,
should I cook for my dog?

00:07:10.221 --> 00:07:14.459
And that is a bad idea
because what we eat

00:07:14.459 --> 00:07:16.594
is not a balanced
diet for a dog.

00:07:16.594 --> 00:07:19.464
And so home-cooked
diets run a huge risk

00:07:19.464 --> 00:07:21.666
of being deficient
in nutrients

00:07:21.666 --> 00:07:23.501
that are critical
for your dog,

00:07:23.501 --> 00:07:26.237
mostly deficient in
calcium phosphorus.

00:07:26.237 --> 00:07:29.340
So cooking for your dog,
unless you've had a diet

00:07:29.340 --> 00:07:32.176
made by a board-certified
veterinary nutritionist,

00:07:32.176 --> 00:07:33.311
is a bad idea.

00:07:33.311 --> 00:07:36.547
"Dog mouths are cleaner
than human mouths."

00:07:36.547 --> 00:07:38.649
No.
Human bites are

00:07:38.649 --> 00:07:39.951
supposedly worse,

00:07:39.951 --> 00:07:41.853
although we don't see
a lot of human bites

00:07:41.853 --> 00:07:43.221
at the
Animal Medical Center.

00:07:43.221 --> 00:07:45.523
But all of us have
been bitten by a dog,

00:07:45.523 --> 00:07:46.858
and we know that those

00:07:46.858 --> 00:07:48.493
can get really
badly infected.

00:07:48.493 --> 00:07:50.495
What I've read
is that if a

00:07:50.495 --> 00:07:52.129
human bites another human,

00:07:52.129 --> 00:07:55.666
it's awful, it's a terrible
infection that you get.

00:07:55.666 --> 00:08:00.071
So, no, I don't know
that either is better.

00:08:00.071 --> 00:08:01.405
Fox: Dog mouths
and human mouths

00:08:01.405 --> 00:08:02.240
are actually,

00:08:02.240 --> 00:08:03.140
it's interesting,

00:08:03.140 --> 00:08:04.475
they're sort of similar.

00:08:04.475 --> 00:08:06.577
Both humans and dogs
have over 200 types

00:08:06.577 --> 00:08:07.845
of bacteria in their mouth.

00:08:07.845 --> 00:08:10.014
However, because

00:08:10.014 --> 00:08:12.383
disease is not
really transmissible

00:08:12.383 --> 00:08:13.584
between dogs and humans,

00:08:13.584 --> 00:08:15.686
or there's not that
much zoonotic potential

00:08:15.686 --> 00:08:17.989
between dog saliva
and human saliva,

00:08:17.989 --> 00:08:19.991
they're clean in that,
you know,

00:08:19.991 --> 00:08:21.359
making out with your dog,

00:08:21.359 --> 00:08:23.294
you won't contract
the common cold,

00:08:23.294 --> 00:08:25.863
but making out with a
person, perhaps you can.

00:08:25.863 --> 00:08:28.432
So, in that case, they
sort of are cleaner.

00:08:28.432 --> 00:08:31.569
However, they still have
tons and tons of bacteria,

00:08:31.569 --> 00:08:33.070
just like people mouths do.

00:08:33.070 --> 00:08:35.907
Hohenhaus: "Dogs are
afraid of their reflection."

00:08:35.907 --> 00:08:37.308
This is...just no.

00:08:37.308 --> 00:08:40.011
Just Google
"puppies in mirrors,"

00:08:40.011 --> 00:08:41.913
and you'll see
that dogs love,

00:08:41.913 --> 00:08:44.916
they're so enamored
of that other dog

00:08:44.916 --> 00:08:46.651
that they never met before.

00:08:46.651 --> 00:08:48.019
They're really cute.

00:08:48.019 --> 00:08:50.655
"Dogs can't digest grains."

00:08:50.655 --> 00:08:52.189
So, this is a major,
major myth.

00:08:52.189 --> 00:08:55.259
Dogs can absolutely
digest grains.

00:08:55.259 --> 00:08:57.161
And not only can
they digest grains,

00:08:57.161 --> 00:08:59.163
but recent research shows

00:08:59.163 --> 00:09:01.265
that they need
grain in their diet.

00:09:01.265 --> 00:09:03.935
Dogs on a grain-free
diet are predisposed to

00:09:03.935 --> 00:09:06.003
developing a very serious
type of heart disease

00:09:06.003 --> 00:09:08.806
called DCM, or
dilated cardiomyopathy.

00:09:08.806 --> 00:09:13.010
And dogs that are on
a grain-inclusive diet

00:09:13.010 --> 00:09:15.713
are not as likely to
develop this heart disease.

00:09:15.713 --> 00:09:17.682
So if your dog is on
a grain-free diet,

00:09:17.682 --> 00:09:20.718
it absolutely needs
grain back in its diet,

00:09:20.718 --> 00:09:21.385
and you should speak 

00:09:21.385 --> 00:09:23.120
to your veterinarian
about doing so.

00:09:23.120 --> 00:09:25.222
"Putting a dog's
nose in their mess

00:09:25.222 --> 00:09:26.991
will break a bad habit."

00:09:26.991 --> 00:09:28.626
This is definitely a myth.

00:09:28.626 --> 00:09:31.696
I think this is,
like, a training method

00:09:31.696 --> 00:09:33.965
that people introduced
a long time ago.

00:09:33.965 --> 00:09:36.734
However, I think with a
lot of research and time

00:09:36.734 --> 00:09:38.703
we found out that
negative reinforcement

00:09:38.703 --> 00:09:40.204
is very bad for dogs,

00:09:40.204 --> 00:09:42.106
definitely doesn't
correct bad behavior

00:09:42.106 --> 00:09:44.208
and can actually
breed bad behavior,

00:09:44.208 --> 00:09:47.511
as well as breeding
aggressive behavior

00:09:47.511 --> 00:09:48.913
and fear in our pets,

00:09:48.913 --> 00:09:49.780
and that's definitely not

00:09:49.780 --> 00:09:50.648
something that
we wanna do.

00:09:50.648 --> 00:09:52.717
Ideally you should
always reward your dog

00:09:52.717 --> 00:09:55.886
with positive behavior,
like treats or playtime

00:09:55.886 --> 00:09:58.356
or feeding rather
than punishing,

00:09:58.356 --> 00:10:01.359
which is never
recommended in dogs.

00:10:01.359 --> 00:10:03.427
Hohenhaus: So, we talked
about dog myths today.

00:10:03.427 --> 00:10:05.963
And it turns out there
are a lot of myths.

00:10:05.963 --> 00:10:07.932
In fact,
Dr. Fox and I didn't agree

00:10:07.932 --> 00:10:11.335
with hardly any of the cues
that we were given today

00:10:11.335 --> 00:10:13.738
because most
of them are myths.

00:10:13.738 --> 00:10:15.139
So beware of Dr. Google.

00:10:15.139 --> 00:10:17.208
Beware of what
your friends tell you,

00:10:17.208 --> 00:10:19.910
or the nice passerby on
the street who tells you

00:10:19.910 --> 00:10:22.246
you're not taking care of
your dog the right way.

00:10:22.246 --> 00:10:25.816
And get an expert opinion
when it comes to your dog.

